<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # The Importance of Water – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Importance of Water</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How We Use Water Everyday</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Follow instructions for academic tasks or projects. Reading: Extract key facts and details from informational texts. Phonemic Awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by applying knowledge of basic syllabification rules when decoding two-syllable, three-syllable, or compound words.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can follow instructions related to water conservation (e.g., "We need to save water every day.")<br>Can extract key details from texts about the importance of water (e.g., "If we don't keep water clean, people will get sick. Clean water is important for health.")<br>Can demonstrate phonemic awareness by applying basic syllabification rules when decoding two-syllable, three-syllable, or compound words (e.g., "forecast," "important").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 30%; text-align: left;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%; text-align: left;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 20%; text-align: left;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays flashcards or pictures related to different uses of water (e.g., drinking, cooking, cleaning, showering, watering plants). Students quickly call out how they use water everyday, using vocabulary from Lesson 1. The teacher writes their ideas on the board in a mind map format, focusing on verbs like "drink," "cook," "clean," "wash," "water."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces the concept of reading for specific information. "Today, we will read about why water is important and how to save it. When we read, it's important to find key words." The teacher points to a simple instruction on the board (e.g., "Circle the verbs.") and models how to read and follow it, highlighting the action. The teacher also models the pronunciation of key compound words from the previous lesson, like "rainwater" and "forecast," emphasizing the stress on the first syllable and guiding students through basic syllabification (e.g., "rain-wa-ter," "fore-cast," "im-por-tant").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is our main goal today? Are we writing or reading?" (Expected answer: Reading.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'circle the word water,' what should you do?" (Expected answer: Draw a circle around 'water'.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "The Importance of Water."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short informational text titled "Our Daily Water Use and Why We Must Save It." The text is a simple explanatory paragraph suitable for A2.2, incorporating the target grammar (first conditional, for + verb + ing, because + cause) and vocabulary (water, resource, forecast, shortage, tap, filter, save, drink, waste, conserve, important, potable, daily).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Example Text:</i><br><b>"Our Daily Water Use and Why We Must Save It"</b><br>We use water daily for many important things. For example, we use water for drinking, for cooking, and for cleaning our homes. It is an essential resource for everyone. However, water is a limited resource, and we must conserve it carefully. If we don't save water, we will face water shortages in the future. If the weather forecast shows little rain, it's even more important to turn off the tap when not using water. We save water because it is a vital resource for our health and for our planet. When rainwater is collected, we can use it for watering plants, helping to save potable water. Remember, every drop counts!</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Extracting key facts and details from informational texts about water conservation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the text silently for gist. Teacher asks: "What is this text mainly about?" (Expected: Water and saving it.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher provides True/False statements related to the text for specific information, e.g., "We use water for sleeping. (False)", "Water is a limited resource. (True)". Students read and circle T or F.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading for specific details using target vocabulary and grammar (First conditional, for + verb + ing, because + cause). Applying basic syllabification rules.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Reading and Gap-fill):</b> Students receive a worksheet with sentences from the text, but with key vocabulary words missing. They read the sentences and fill in the blanks using a word bank (e.g., <i>save, important, shortages, filter, tap, forecast</i>). Example: "If we don't ______ water, we will face water ______." (save, shortages). This reinforces vocabulary and text comprehension.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Completion & Syllabification):</b> Students read incomplete sentences based on the text and complete them, focusing on the target grammar. Example: "We use water for ___________ and __________." (drinking, cooking). "We save water because ___________." (it is a limited resource). Teacher then guides students to locate and underline words like "forecast" and "important" in the text. Students practice applying basic syllabification rules to these words orally and visually (e.g., clapping syllables: fore-cast, im-por-tant).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction / Peer Check (Instructions and Detail Hunt):</b> In pairs, students read a set of simple instructions. One instruction is: "Find all sentences in the text that use 'if' and underline them." Another: "Circle all the verbs that explain how we use water (e.g., drinking, cooking, cleaning)." They work together to follow these reading instructions and then check their answers with another pair.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "The Importance of Water."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a "Water Conservation Tip Card" with 2-3 short, clear instructions related to saving water. They must read these instructions carefully and then collaboratively create a simple, illustrated poster summarizing these tips. For example, one card might say: "1. Turn off the tap while washing hands. 2. Collect rainwater for plants." Students will draw a simple picture for each tip and write the instruction clearly on the poster. They will use the target vocabulary and grammar structures learned from the text, such as "If you turn off the tap, you will save water." or "We use rainwater for watering plants." This task requires close reading of instructions and extracting key information for a practical application.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during poster creation and presentation of tips. Peer assessment where groups provide feedback on clarity of instructions on other groups' posters.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group briefly presents their poster to the class, reading aloud their conservation tips and explaining their illustrations. The teacher observes if students correctly followed the instructions on their tip cards and effectively communicated the water-saving message.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in following written instructions from the tip card.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and legibility of written tips on the poster.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of at least two target vocabulary words related to water conservation (e.g., <i>save, tap, rainwater</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Basic intelligibility of pronunciation when reading tips aloud (especially for words like "important," "forecast" if they use them).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration within the group during poster creation.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a short exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can follow instructions to save water." (Yes/No/A little)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I used today about water is ______."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most important thing you read about water today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can you use what you learned today to help save water at home?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in engaging students with the reading tasks and vocabulary? Were the instructions clear for the poster activity? What adjustments are needed, especially for reinforcing syllabification or specific grammar points in future reading tasks? Did students successfully extract key facts and follow instructions? For example, perhaps more visual aids are needed for complex words like "sanitation" or "drought" in future texts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction), students will use the facts and conservation tips they read and understood today to discuss and propose solutions for water conservation with their peers, moving from reading about conservation to actively speaking about it.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>